// A KNOWN, DELIBERATELY UNCLOSED divergence, not a checkpoint that both
// sides must agree on. down(n) holds 20 locals plus its callee and argument
// slots (22 value-stack slots per frame). At n=900 that is 900 * 22 =
// 19800 value-stack slots, past src/vm.h's STACK_MAX (16384), while the
// call chain itself (about 902 frames) stays well under FRAMES_MAX (1024).
//
// Native rejects this with "Stack overflow." (the STACK_MAX guard in
// Runtime::push) and exit 70, the same controlled path FRAMES_MAX uses. The
// JVM backend mirrors FRAMES_MAX (runtime/jvm/src/lox/LoxClosure.java) but
// has no counterpart for STACK_MAX -- it counts calls, not value-stack
// slots -- so this same program runs to completion on the JVM backend and
// prints 210, exit 0.
//
// tools/check_jvm_probes.sh's known_divergence_probes checks this file
// directly. It asserts that the divergence stays exactly this shape (native
// fails via STACK_MAX, the JVM backend succeeds), so a change to either
// side's guard is caught, not silently absorbed. The JVM backend having no
// ceiling to match native's own is tracked outside this file.
//
// Lives one level below jvm-only/, not directly in it: tools/diff_runtimes.py's
// CI step walks test/translation-probes/ and test/translation-probes/jvm-only/
// non-recursively and requires every program's stdout to match (or, for an
// empty-stdout failure on both sides, the exit status to agree on success
// against failure). This probe's two sides disagree on both counts by
// design, so a generic byte-for-byte walk must never reach it;
// check_jvm_probes.sh names it directly instead.
fun down(n) {
    var a = 1; var b = 2; var c = 3; var d = 4; var e = 5;
    var g = 6; var h = 7; var i = 8; var j = 9; var k = 10;
    var l = 11; var m = 12; var o = 13; var p = 14; var q = 15;
    var r = 16; var s = 17; var t = 18; var u = 19; var v = 20;
    if (n == 0) return a+b+c+d+e+g+h+i+j+k+l+m+o+p+q+r+s+t+u+v;
    down(n - 1);
    return a+b+c+d+e+g+h+i+j+k+l+m+o+p+q+r+s+t+u+v;
}
print down(900);
